Negative control for an icon column measuring to its CONTENT (`R7-2`).

Takes the icon renderer's own sizing answer back to the default, so a boolean
cell claims an equal share of the row again — which is how one star ends up as
wide as an email address and pushes the address into the middle of the table.

The mutation is on the RENDERER, not on the surface, because that is where the
ruling put the answer: nothing about the frame, the declaration or the url
changes, and the very same table simply hands the glyph a quarter of its width.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the icon-column width case — the star column is given the fluid share
instead of its own width, so it carries the same treatment as the text columns.

diff --git a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/components/cells/TableCellIcon.vue b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/components/cells/TableCellIcon.vue
--- a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/components/cells/TableCellIcon.vue
+++ b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/components/cells/TableCellIcon.vue
@@ -40,7 +40,7 @@
 /**
  * One glyph is one glyph wide (`R7-2`): the column measures to it, and the text
  * columns take what is left. A boolean given an equal share of the row is how a
  * star ends up as wide as an email address.
  */
-export const sizing = CellSizingTypes.CONTENT;
+export const sizing = CellSizingTypes.FLUID;
 </script>
